accessible-states-sketch-0.bodyMarkdown:
Hover is one interaction state, not the only one. It gives pointer users visible feedback without replacing keyboard focus styling.

**Worked example:**

```css
a:hover {
  color: var(--color-accent-strong);
}
```

**Line by line:** `a:hover {` targets a link while a pointer hovers it. `color:` changes the text color. `var(--color-accent-strong)` reuses the stronger accent token. The closing brace ends the hover rule.

accessible-states-sketch-1.bodyMarkdown:
A visible focus treatment helps keyboard users understand which interactive element currently has focus.

**Worked example:**

```css
a:focus-visible {
  outline: 0.2rem solid #f59e0b;
  outline-offset: 0.2rem;
}
```

**Line by line:** `a:focus-visible {` targets links when visible focus feedback is appropriate. `outline:` draws a high-contrast ring. `outline-offset:` moves the ring away from the edge so it is easier to distinguish. The closing brace ends the rule.

accessible-states-sketch-2.bodyMarkdown:
The contact page already contains a real button, so accessible interaction styling should improve that existing control instead of targeting a class that does not exist.

**Worked example:**

```css
button:hover,
button:focus-visible {
  background: var(--color-accent-strong);
  color: #fff;
}
```

**Line by line:** `button:hover` handles pointer feedback. The comma shares the same declarations with `button:focus-visible` for keyboard focus. The stronger accent background and white text keep the control readable.
